From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jrash <johnrash@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 02:13:16 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 045/342] meson_hdmi: video overlay on the VPU's VD1 layer

One overlay channel for B_YCbCr422 buffers of up to 2048x2048:

- Buffers are physically contiguous pages, mapped write combining into
  the kernel's address space with user access and B_CLONEABLE_AREA, like
  the frame buffer: the one address works in app_server, in the client
  drawing into the overlay bitmap, and in a media_server clone.
- The VD1 read interface takes the visible part of the buffer through
  canvas 0x60 or 0x61 (in turn, so a new frame replaces the shown one at
  the start of a frame: no tearing at 30 fps), the formatter expands
  4:2:2, the VPP scaler scales it (4.24 fixed point steps, 1/4 to 64x),
  and the postblender puts it under OSD1.
- Keying: the video shows where the frame buffer's alpha is 0. OSD1
  normally replaces every alpha with 0xff; while the video layer is on,
  the frame buffer's own alpha counts. app_server fills the overlay with
  its key colour, whose alpha is 0, and draws everything else opaque.
  OSD1's colour key registers do nothing for 32 bit pixels.
- KDL: "meson_hdmi overlay" shows the state, "meson_hdmi vpu <word>
  [<value>]" reads or writes a VPU register.

Register facts are from the S905X datasheet (VD1, VPP); the programming
sequence follows Linux's meson_overlay.c (GPL), read for facts only.

Tested on the ODROID-C2: 1280x720 colour bars scaled to 960x540 and
1080p H.264 in MediaPlayer scaled to its window, both 1080p60 output;
1080p30 playback takes 35% of the CPU instead of 61%.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
